\section{Impulse Response Function (IRF): Full Expanded Derivation}
% =============================================================================

\subsection{Dynamic LP Model at Horizon $h$}

To trace how outcomes respond \emph{over time} to a shock, we extend the
static model to horizon $h = 0, 1, 2, \ldots, H$.  At each horizon $h$,
the $h$-period-ahead cumulative change in outcomes is:

\begin{keybox}[LP at Horizon $h$]
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LP_h}
  \underbrace{\mathbf{Y}_{t+h} - \mathbf{Y}_{t-1}}_{2\times1}
  \;=\;
  \bm{\alpha}_h
  \;+\;
  \underbrace{A_{h}}_{2\times5}
  \;\underbrace{\Omega}_{5\times5}
  \;\underbrace{\bm{\varepsilon}_{t}}_{5\times1}
  \;+\;
  \underbrace{\mathbf{u}_{t+h}}_{2\times1},
  \qquad h = 0,1,\ldots,H
\end{equation}
\end{keybox}

\noindent where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $A_{h} \in \R^{2\times5}$ is the \textbf{horizon-$h$ response matrix}
        (estimated separately for each $h$),
  \item $\Omega \in \R^{5\times5}$ is the \textbf{fixed weight matrix} (same
        across all horizons, estimated once),
  \item $\mathbf{u}_{t+h}$ is the LP residual, uncorrelated with $\bm{\varepsilon}_t$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{IRF Definition}

The \textbf{Impulse Response of outcome $y_{i}$ to shock $\varepsilon^{k}$
at horizon $h$} is:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:IRF_def}
  \mathrm{IRF}_{i,k}(h)
  \;\equiv\;
  \frac{\partial\, y_{i,t+h}}{\partial\, \varepsilon_{t}^{k}}
  \;=\;
  [A_{h}\,\Omega]_{ik}
  \;=\;
  \sum_{j=1}^{5} a_{ij}^{(h)}\,\omega_{jk},
  \quad i=1,2;\; k=1,\ldots,5.
\end{equation}

The full \textbf{$2\times5$ IRF matrix at horizon $h$} is therefore:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:IRF_matrix}
  \underbrace{\mathbf{IRF}(h)}_{2\times5}
  \;=\;
  \underbrace{A_{h}}_{2\times5}\;\underbrace{\Omega}_{5\times5}
\end{equation}

\subsection{Full Expansion of $A_h\,\Omega$: Every Entry Visible}

Writing the product out completely with no shorthand:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:IRF_full}
\mathbf{IRF}(h)
=
\begin{pmatrix}
  a_{11}^{(h)} & a_{12}^{(h)} & a_{13}^{(h)} & a_{14}^{(h)} & a_{15}^{(h)}\\[6pt]
  a_{21}^{(h)} & a_{22}^{(h)} & a_{23}^{(h)} & a_{24}^{(h)} & a_{25}^{(h)}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
  \omega_{11} & \omega_{12} & \omega_{13} & \omega_{14} & \omega_{15}\\[4pt]
  \omega_{21} & \omega_{22} & \omega_{23} & \omega_{24} & \omega_{25}\\[4pt]
  \omega_{31} & \omega_{32} & \omega_{33} & \omega_{34} & \omega_{35}\\[4pt]
  \omega_{41} & \omega_{42} & \omega_{43} & \omega_{44} & \omega_{45}\\[4pt]
  \omega_{51} & \omega_{52} & \omega_{53} & \omega_{54} & \omega_{55}
\end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}

The resulting $2\times5$ matrix in full, with every multiplication visible:

