\subsection{Reading the IRF: What Each Term Captures}

Each term $a_{ij}^{(h)}\,\omega_{jk}$ in the IRF decomposition has a precise
two-layer economic interpretation:

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.6}
\begin{tabular}{cp{5.5cm}p{5.5cm}}
\toprule
\textbf{Factor} & \textbf{Economic meaning} & \textbf{Role in IRF} \\
\midrule
$\omega_{jk}$ &
  How much aggregate channel $j$ is activated by structural shock $k$ &
  First layer: shock $\to$ aggregate component \\
$a_{ij}^{(h)}$ &
  How much outcome $y_i$ responds to aggregate component $j$ at horizon $h$ &
  Second layer: aggregate component $\to$ outcome, with dynamics \\
$a_{ij}^{(h)}\omega_{jk}$ &
  Contribution of channel $j$ to the total response of $y_i$ to shock $k$ at $h$ &
  One pathway in the transmission mechanism \\
$\sum_{j=1}^{5}a_{ij}^{(h)}\omega_{jk}$ &
  Total response of $y_i$ to shock $k$ at horizon $h$, summing all five channels &
  The IRF: $\mathrm{IRF}_{i,k}(h)$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Cumulative IRF}

The \textbf{cumulative IRF} up to horizon $H$ sums responses across all horizons:

\begin{equation}\label{eq:CIRF}
  \mathrm{CIRF}_{i,k}(H)
  = \sum_{h=0}^{H} \mathrm{IRF}_{i,k}(h)
  = \sum_{h=0}^{H} \sum_{j=1}^{5} a_{ij}^{(h)}\,\omega_{jk}
  = \sum_{j=1}^{5} \omega_{jk} \left(\sum_{h=0}^{H} a_{ij}^{(h)}\right).
\end{equation}

\noindent The factorization on the right separates the weight matrix $\Omega$
(estimated once) from the horizon-by-horizon dynamic response $\sum_h a_{ij}^{(h)}$
(estimated by LP), providing a clean decomposition of the long-run effect.

