\section{Simulation Study I: Scalar Aggregate LP vs.\ Direct LP}
% =============================================================================

\subsection{The Root Cause of Difference}

The Direct LP and the Scalar Aggregate LP estimate fundamentally different
objects.  The distinction is not a rotation---it is a \textbf{model
restriction}.

\begin{center}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.6}
\begin{tabular}{p{3.5cm} p{5cm} p{5cm}}
\toprule
& \textbf{Direct LP} & \textbf{Scalar Aggregate LP} \\
\midrule
\textbf{Regressors} & $\bm{\varepsilon}_t \in\R^K$ (5 regressors) & $s_t = \hat{\omega}^{\top}\bm{\varepsilon}_t \in \R$ (1 regressor) \\
\textbf{Free parameters per horizon} & $N\times K = 10$ & $N\times 1 = 2$ \\
\textbf{IRF for shock $k$ at $h$} & $\hat{\gamma}_{ik}^{(h)}$ — free coefficient & $\hat{\beta}_i^{(h)}\cdot\hat{\omega}_k$ — product \\
\textbf{Shape of IRF across $h$} & \textbf{Different} per shock (unrestricted) & \textbf{Same shape} for all shocks (scaled) \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent The scalar aggregate LP imposes the hard restriction:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:prop_constraint}
  \frac{\mathrm{IRF}_{i,k}(h)}{\mathrm{IRF}_{i,k'}(h)}
  = \frac{\hat{\omega}_k}{\hat{\omega}_{k'}}
  \quad = \text{constant for all } h.
\end{equation}
All shocks are forced to have the \emph{same time profile}, differing only in
scale.  The Direct LP imposes no such constraint; each shock can decay at its
own rate.

\subsection{Data-Generating Process}

We simulate $T=600$ observations where each shock drives its own
AR($\rho_k$) factor process:
\[
  Z_{k,t} = \rho_k Z_{k,t-1} + \varepsilon_{k,t},
  \qquad
  \mathbf{Y}_t = \sum_{k=1}^{5} Z_{k,t}\,\alpha_k + \bm{\omega}_t.
\]
The \textbf{key design}: shocks have deliberately different persistence:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Shock} & \textbf{Persistence $\rho_k$} & \textbf{Impact on log IP ($\alpha_k$)} \\
\midrule
FP     & $0.90$ (very persistent) & $+0.50$ \\
MP     & $0.45$ (fast decay)      & $-0.35$ \\
TFP    & $0.75$ (medium)          & $+0.40$ \\
Oil    & $0.55$ (moderately fast) & $-0.25$ \\
Crisis & $0.20$ (one-period spike)& $-0.70$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent The true IRF of $\log\mathrm{IP}$ to shock $k$ is:
$\mathrm{IRF}_{k}(h) = \alpha_k\,\rho_k^h$, which has a \textbf{different
time shape} for every shock.  This is exactly what the scalar aggregate LP
cannot capture.

\subsection{Estimation}

\textbf{Direct LP} regresses $\Delta_h\mathbf{Y}_{t+h}$ on all five shocks
$\bm{\varepsilon}_t$ separately (10 coefficients per horizon).

\textbf{Scalar Aggregate LP} (two steps):
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Extract the first principal component $\hat{\omega}\in\R^{5}$ from $\bm{\varepsilon}_t$.
  \item Compute $s_t = \hat{\omega}^{\top}\bm{\varepsilon}_t\in\R$ (scalar).
  \item Run LP of $\Delta_h\mathbf{Y}_{t+h}$ on $s_t$ alone (2 coefficients per horizon).
  \item $\mathrm{IRF}_{i,k}(h) = \hat{\beta}_i^{(h)}\cdot\hat{\omega}_k$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Results: Genuine Differences in IRF Estimates}

Table~\ref{tab:irf_both} reports the $\log\mathrm{IP}$ IRF at key horizons
for each shock.

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
