    ($\rho_{\mathrm{MP}}=0.45 \ll \rho_{\mathrm{FP}}=0.90$).
    \textbf{Scalar Agg LP} (red): ratio is \emph{exactly flat}
    (fixed at $\hat\omega_k/\hat\omega_{\mathrm{FP}}$), revealing the
    hard constraint the model imposes.
    The flat red line against the varying blue line is direct evidence
    that the scalar aggregate LP misspecifies the IRF dynamics.}
  \label{fig:channels}
\end{figure}

\begin{keybox}[Why the Two IRFs Must Differ]
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Different shock persistence ($\rho_k$ differs)}: The true
        ratio $\mathrm{IRF}_k(h)/\mathrm{IRF}_{k'}(h)
        = (\alpha_k/\alpha_{k'})\,(\rho_k/\rho_{k'})^h$ changes
        exponentially in $h$.  The scalar aggregate LP forces this ratio
        to be $\hat\omega_k/\hat\omega_{k'}$, a constant---a
        constraint that is generically false.

  \item \textbf{PCA ignores $Y$}: The first PC $\hat\omega$ is estimated
        from $\bm\varepsilon_t$ alone, without using $Y_t$.  It maximises
        variance of the shock series, \emph{not} explanatory power for $Y$.
        For i.i.d.\ shocks all PCs have equal variance, so the first PC
        is an \emph{arbitrary} direction in $\R^K$, leading to
        severely distorted IRFs for shocks that happen to lie off this
        direction (FP: $\hat\omega_k=-0.036$, nearly orthogonal to $s_t$).

  \item \textbf{Degrees of freedom}: Direct LP has $N\!\times\!K=10$ free
        IRF parameters per horizon; scalar aggregate LP has only $N=2$.
        The 8-parameter reduction imposes constraints that are violated
        in the data.
\end{enumerate}
\textbf{Conclusion}: Direct LP and Scalar Aggregate LP produce the same
IRFs only in the special case where all shocks have identical time profiles
(same $\rho_k$) \emph{and} the first PC aligns with the true aggregate index.
In general---and in this simulation---they are substantially different.
\end{keybox}

