\section{Simulation Study II: Ridge LP Breaks the OLS Identity}
\label{sec:ridge_sim}
% =============================================================================

We now demonstrate the OLS identity and Ridge divergence numerically.

\subsection{Data-Generating Process}

We use the same structural DGP as Section~9, with one addition: a
non-orthogonal mixing matrix $\Omega$.

\begin{align}
  &\bm{\varepsilon}_t \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, I_5), \quad
  Z_{k,t} = \rho_k Z_{k,t-1} + \varepsilon_{k,t}, \nonumber\\[4pt]
  &\mathbf{Y}_t = \alpha\,Z_t + 0.3\,\bm{\eta}_t, \qquad
  \mathbf{s}_t = \Omega\,\bm{\varepsilon}_t.
  \label{eq:dgp_ridge}
\end{align}

\noindent Parameters: $T=500$, $K=5$, $H_{\max}=20$, $N_Y=2$.

\medskip\noindent
Persistence: $\rho = (0.90,\; 0.45,\; 0.75,\; 0.55,\; 0.20)$.

\medskip\noindent
Impact matrix $\alpha$ ($2\times5$):
\[
\alpha = \begin{pmatrix}
  +0.50 & -0.35 & +0.40 & -0.25 & -0.70 \\
  -0.20 & +0.15 & -0.10 & +0.30 & +0.50
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\noindent
Non-orthogonal mixing matrix $\Omega$ ($5\times5$):
\[
\Omega = \begin{pmatrix}
  0.80 &  0.15 & -0.10 &  0.05 &  0.20 \\
  0.10 &  0.70 &  0.20 & -0.15 &  0.10 \\
 -0.05 &  0.10 &  0.85 &  0.10 & -0.05 \\
  0.15 & -0.20 &  0.05 &  0.75 &  0.15 \\
  0.10 &  0.05 &  0.15 &  0.10 &  0.60
\end{pmatrix},
\quad
\max|\Omega\Omega' - I| = 0.5950,\;
\det(\Omega)=0.166.
\]

\subsection{Estimation}

At each horizon $h=0,1,\ldots,20$, we estimate both models:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Direct LP}: $\hat{\Gamma}_h = (E'E + \lambda I)^{-1}E'\mathbf{Y}_{+h}$,
        using $E = [\bm{\varepsilon}_1,\ldots,\bm{\varepsilon}_T]'$.
  \item \textbf{Aggregate LP}: $\hat{A}_h = (S'S + \lambda I)^{-1}S'\mathbf{Y}_{+h}$,
        using $S = E\Omega'$.  Convert to structural:
        $\hat{\Gamma}_h^{\mathrm{Agg}} = \hat{A}_h\,\Omega$.
\end{enumerate}

\noindent We run both with $\lambda=0$ (OLS) and $\lambda=500$ (Ridge).
The Ridge penalty $\lambda=500$ produces approximately 50\% shrinkage since
$E'E \approx TI_K = 500I_5$.

